BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 381.77 Tm (The first sponsor-side trap is the stale contract. Gary Alton watched a multi-year deal freeze a client into "stale) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 366.55 Tm (discounts," with one exit penalty topping $500K, and in the same case study a carrier negotiated 75% but passed along) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 351.32 Tm (65%, quietly keeping a 10% spread [Cxvmh46o824 @ 00:44:25]. Alton puts typical audit findings at 10-30%, the size) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 336.10 Tm (of leak a frozen contract can hide. Alan Pannier explains why the lock hurts even without fraud: over the term the PBM) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 320.87 Tm (keeps winning better retail pharmacy and manufacturer pricing, but the sponsor gets none of those gains because its) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 305.65 Tm (pricing is fixed [ZuvAxUS0mII @ 00:01:02]. Justine at TheCoyPharmer flags the trap inside the guarantee itself: PBM) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 290.42 Tm (guarantees to plan sponsors are "typically a minimum level," so a sponsor promised AWP minus 89% that runs at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 275.20 Tm (minus 88% gets the shortfall paid [H3p5Yw0Y-ek @ 00:07:20]. The catch is that the PBM runs separate MAC lists per) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 259.97 Tm (guarantee, groups sponsors into 5-8 cohorts, adjusts the lists daily or weekly, and may migrate an overperforming) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 244.75 Tm (sponsor onto a new MAC list [H3p5Yw0Y-ek @ 00:13:52]. The economics check is simple: without the monthly or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 229.52 Tm (weekly reporting Justine tells resellers to demand, a sponsor cannot tell whether its contractual floor quietly became its) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 214.30 Tm (ceiling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 199.07 Tm (On rebates, the trap is believing the pass-through promise without seeing the plumbing. Frier Levitt describes litigation) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 183.85 Tm (that exposed PBM-owned rebate aggregators demanding, say, 25 cents on the dollar for formulary access, keeping) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 168.62 Tm (about 30% and sending 60-70 cents to the PBM, which then tells the plan "we're going to turn it all over to you") Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 153.40 Tm ([8mubYuuHvMI @ 00:01:20]. Frier Levitt adds that Medicare Part D and Medicare Advantage carry reporting duties) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 138.17 Tm (while ERISA private plans have no such obligation, so the private employer sees only what the contract forces) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 122.95 Tm ([8mubYuuHvMI @ 00:02:16]. Benefits Blake puts a number on the damage: only 59% of employers receive full) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 107.72 Tm (traditional rebates and 57% full specialty rebates [wUzyX8bEvt0 @ 00:11:06]. The second trap is the referee. Frier) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 92.50 Tm (Levitt calls consultants paid by the PBM a "real conflict of interest," and Cora Opsahl's panel describes big PBMs) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 77.27 Tm (paying consultants per RFP while renewals get pitched as "only up 4% or 6%" [xHAyQelFkf8 @ 00:16:01]. Even the) Tj ET
